course: dictionary
lesson: 11157
title: Win: one word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: The word win can mean different things. The sentence around it tells you which meaning to use. «Letting a small child win sometimes is kind, not cheating.» Here win means to come first in a game, so the child gets to be the one who finishes ahead.
explain: Try your meaning in the sentence. «Children can share their wins with other grown-ups, like a teacher, a grandparent or a coach.» Swap in "games they won" and it still makes sense, so here a win is a game or match that you won.
explain: Sometimes win means to succeed or get the better of something. «Other reef animals win by tricks» means they succeed, and stay safe, by using clever tricks. When you are not sure, read the words around win and ask which meaning fits.
fact: Win is a verb and a noun: you can win a game, or have a win.
fact: The verb forms are win, wins, won and winning.
fact: «Win» means to be the best or come first in a game, race or competition.
fact: «Win a prize» means to get a prize because you did well or were lucky.
fact: «Win someone over» means to get them to agree with you or like you.
exercise: «Letting a small child win sometimes is kind, not cheating.» What does win mean here? | To make them very tired | To buy them a prize | To sing loudly | *To come first in a game | To let them come first in a game
exercise: «England's women's football team, the Lionesses, won the European Championship in 2022.» What does won mean? | Was given a book for free | Shared a cup with a friend | *Came first in a competition | Lost badly
exercise: Ali says, «I won a prize in the raffle.» What does this mean? | He lost the prize | He made the raffle | *He got a prize because he was lucky or did well | He gave the prize away
test: In «Her win was on sports day», is win a verb or a noun? | Adverb | Adjective | Verb | *Noun
test: What does «win someone over» mean? | To give them a prize | To beat them at a game | To run past them | *To get them to agree with you or like you
exam: Which meaning fits «a win-win»? | Two wins in one day | A tie between two teams | Losing twice | *Good for everyone involved
